{
  "module": "Input side: text to vectors",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "subwords-bpe",
    "title": "Splitting text: subwords and byte-pair encoding",
    "summary": "Why models split text into subwords, how byte-pair encoding builds a vocabulary by merging frequent pairs, what byte-level guarantees, and what tokens-not-characters costs.",
    "covers": [
      "subword-tokenization",
      "bpe"
    ],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "tokens",
        "reason": "BPE builds the tokenizer's vocabulary, so tokens, the vocabulary and the tokenizer must already be clear."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "two-ways",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Before a model can read text, something has to decide "
            },
            {
              "text": "where to cut it",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". There are two obvious choices, and each has a problem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Whole words.",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " The vocabulary must hold every word: a huge list, and any word not on it (a new name, a typo) has no representation at all."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Single characters.",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "text": " No unknown words at all, because any text is made of characters. But sequences become very long."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 22)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "long",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Long sequences are expensive. Later in the course you will see that the cost of attention grows as "
            },
            {
              "text": "n²",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": double the tokens and the work roughly quadruples."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A word of eight letters is one token as a word but eight tokens as characters. Splitting into characters multiplies n, and n² multiplies the cost much more."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 22)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "subwords",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Subwords",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " sit in between. Frequent words are kept whole as single tokens. Rare words are split into pieces that are themselves tokens."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The result is a moderate vocabulary that can still cover any text, and sequences that are not much longer than the words themselves."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 22)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "bpe",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Byte-pair encoding (BPE)",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " is a common way to build a subword vocabulary. It is a counting procedure:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": true,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Start with single symbols (characters or bytes)."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Find the most frequent adjacent pair in the training text."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Merge that pair into one new symbol."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Repeat until the vocabulary reaches the size you want."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 23)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "worked",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A tiny example. The training text has these words, with how often each appears: "
            },
            {
              "text": "hug",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (10), "
            },
            {
              "text": "pug",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (5), "
            },
            {
              "text": "pun",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (12), "
            },
            {
              "text": "bun",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (4), "
            },
            {
              "text": "hugs",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (5). Start with single letters."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Count adjacent pairs: "
            },
            {
              "text": "u g",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " appears in hug, pug and hugs: 10 + 5 + 5 = "
            },
            {
              "text": "20",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". That is the most frequent, more than "
            },
            {
              "text": "u n",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (12 + 4 = 16) or "
            },
            {
              "text": "p u",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (5 + 12 = 17)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "So the first merge makes a new symbol "
            },
            {
              "text": "ug",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ". Counting again, the most frequent pair is now "
            },
            {
              "text": "u n",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (16), then "
            },
            {
              "text": "h ug",
              "italic": true
            },
            {
              "text": " (15) would come next."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 23)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "merges",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Each merge is written down in order. The "
            },
            {
              "text": "ordered list of merges is the tokenizer",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "To encode new text: split it into base symbols, then apply the merges in the order they were learned. Frequent pieces get merged into whole tokens; rare ones stay in small pieces."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 23)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "bytes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Byte-level BPE",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " starts from the 256 possible byte values instead of characters. Any string in any language (or any bytes at all) can be written in bytes, so there is "
            },
            {
              "text": "no unknown-token fallback",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "The vocabulary is 256 base bytes, plus the learned merges, plus any special tokens. GPT-2 has 50,257 tokens: 256 + 50,000 merges + 1 special token (end of text)."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 24)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "costs",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Seeing tokens instead of characters has practical consequences:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            [
              {
                "text": "Letter-level tasks (counting the letters in a word, spelling backwards) are harder, because a whole word may be one token and its spelling is not directly visible."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "How digits are grouped into tokens affects how well numbers are handled."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Context limits and API prices are counted in tokens."
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "text": "Text in languages that were rare in the tokenizer's training data usually needs more tokens per word."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 26)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Why do models tokenize into subwords rather than whole words?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Whole-word vocabularies are huge and cannot represent unseen words; subwords cover any text with a moderate vocabulary"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Rare words are split into pieces that are already in the vocabulary."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Subwords are always faster to type"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Typing has nothing to do with it. The reasons are vocabulary size and unseen words."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Whole words are not made of characters"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Whole words are made of characters; the problem is the size of the list."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Subwords make the vocabulary infinite"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A subword vocabulary is moderate and fixed."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "teaches": [
        "two-ways",
        "subwords"
      ],
      "covers": [
        "subword-tokenization"
      ],
      "variants": [
        {
          "prompt": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "What happens to a rare word under subword tokenization?"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "options": [
            {
              "correct": true,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "It is split into several smaller tokens"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "Rare words become pieces; frequent words stay whole."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "correct": false,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "It is replaced by an unknown-word marker"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "Subword vocabularies can represent any text, so no such marker is needed."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "correct": false,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "It is dropped from the text"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "Nothing is dropped; the word is represented by its pieces."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "correct": false,
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "It is added to the vocabulary as a new token"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ],
              "reason": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "spans": [
                    {
                      "text": "The vocabulary is fixed once it is built."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "What is the main cost of splitting text into single characters?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "options": [
        {
          "correct": true,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Sequences become very long, and attention cost grows as n²"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Many more tokens for the same text, and a cost that grows with the square of n."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The vocabulary becomes enormous"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "A character vocabulary is small. The problem is the length of the sequences."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Unseen words cannot be represented"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Characters handle any word. That is a problem of word-level tokens."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "correct": false,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "The model cannot see spaces"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "reason": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "spans": [
                {
                  "text": "Spaces are just characters here; the problem is sequence length."
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
